---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('omnisend')!;
const b = appBySlug('attentive')!;
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/omnisend-vs-attentive');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/omnisend-vs-attentive');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Attentive SMS-only?",
    "answer": "No. Attentive documents Email and Shopify email subscriber collection. Decide whether it will own email too or integrate with your retained email program."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Omnisend recover carts across channels?",
    "answer": "Its documentation supports recovery links in email, SMS and push; the abandoned-products block is for email. Each channel still needs appropriate eligibility."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should two signup popups run together?",
    "answer": "Assign a primary capture experience and test it on mobile. Concurrent forms can create conflicting offers and duplicate welcome sends."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should acquisition be judged?",
    "answer": "Track eligible subscribers through delivered messages, orders, discounts and opt-outs by source. Signup conversion alone does not establish profitable recovery."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose who owns acquisition, welcome offers and cross-channel recovery before choosing a sender.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Omnisend", "reason": "Coordinate email, SMS and push steps in a shared recovery workflow."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Attentive", "reason": "Evaluate Shopify signup capture and SMS alongside its email offering."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>The first decision happens before abandonment</h2>
<p>A shopper arriving from an Instagram ad sees your signup offer before either platform can recover a cart. Decide who owns that form, the promised incentive, channel permissions and the welcome sequence. Omnisend is a candidate for keeping recovery steps across email, SMS and push together. Attentive is a candidate when evaluating its signup collection and SMS program, with a separate decision on whether to adopt Attentive Email. Neither requires assuming SMS always deserves the first touch.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041854231-Shopify">Attentive’s Shopify guide</a> describes its tag displaying signup units and capturing product views, cart adds and purchases. <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4434114124564-Collect-email-subscribers-from-Shopify">Its email collection guide</a> explains enabling collection and updating a signup unit for email opt-in. Those are acquisition capabilities, not proof that every anonymous visitor becomes recoverable.</p>
<h2>Define one offer and a channel permission map</h2>
<p>Draw the mobile path from ad landing page to form, welcome message, cart and checkout. If an existing popup remains, decide whether it or the new signup unit is primary. Test the closed-form state, keyboard, coupon display and checkout handoff on a small screen. Avoid two overlays competing for the same tap or two welcome flows delivering different incentives. Keep the offer terms consistent with subscription products, bundles and sale exclusions.</p>
<p>Store capture source and channel subscription status separately. Test email-only, SMS-only, dual-subscribed and opted-out customers. A customer entering a phone number at checkout is not the same scenario as one completing your SMS signup. Preserve the evidence needed to explain each subscription and do not infer cross-channel permission from a shared customer profile.</p>
<h2>Compare recovery mechanics rather than channel labels</h2>
<p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/6659889-abandoned-cart-and-abandoned-checkout-automations">Omnisend distinguishes cart and checkout abandonment</a> and supports recovery links in email, SMS and push. Its abandoned-products display block belongs in email. The guide advises against running its reminders alongside store-platform reminders. Map the intended trigger to a real customer event and disable the equivalent Shopify flow before sending.</p>
<p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/1740886-add-sms-to-your-omnisend-automations">Omnisend’s SMS workflow guide</a> documents passing contacts who are not opted into a channel onward to the next step. Verify that setting on each message block so an email-only shopper is not stranded at SMS. With Attentive, decide whether email stays with another provider or moves to Attentive Email; then ask for the actual suppression and event handoff to be demonstrated for that arrangement.</p>
<h2>Assign acquisition and recovery ownership explicitly</h2>
<p>If Omnisend retains email while Attentive owns SMS, assign one owner to each welcome and cart message. Specify how an order, unsubscribe or new checkout prevents a pending reminder in both systems. Do not assume an integration copies every event or suppression instantly. Give someone authority to pause the affected flow when the handoff fails. If one provider owns both channels, still test the same purchase exits and campaign collisions.</p>
<p>Use a fresh test identity from the mobile signup unit. Accept only email, abandon a cart, then repeat with a separate SMS subscriber. Check the cart link, product details, selected size and gift fields. Complete a purchase before the next scheduled message and confirm no further recovery is sent. Record event timestamps and sender logs; these proposed exercises establish configuration behavior, not a vendor-wide performance ranking.</p>
<h2>Use the cart problem to decide the next message</h2>
<p>A financing or BNPL dropout needs payment help, while unexpected international shipping needs a clear delivered-cost explanation. A subscription cart needs frequency and renewal terms. A B2B cart below MOQ needs eligibility guidance. For a partially unavailable size selection, show a valid alternative only if stock and preference justify it; suppress a fully unavailable offer. A repeat buyer may need service information rather than another new-subscriber welcome code.</p>
<p>Use email for details such as sizing or gift delivery information when that is the eligible channel. Use a concise SMS only when permitted and useful, respecting the program’s configured quiet hours and frequency controls. Before an SMS follows email, check the purchase state and other campaigns. An arbitrary three-hour escalation is not evidence of the shopper’s preference or readiness.</p>
<h2>Budget the acquisition offer and recovery offer together</h2>
<p>Hypothetical fixed-goods-cost examples: $317 at 71% gross margin yields $225.07 gross profit. A 13% code costs $41.21, leaving $183.86. At $388 and 42%, gross profit is $162.96; an 11% code costs $42.68, leaving $120.28. Shipping, payment fees, platform costs and returns are excluded. A welcome code plus a recovery code can consume more than either example, so check stacking and bundle exclusions in the actual checkout.</p>
<h2>Measure acquisition quality through the order</h2>
<p>Report by capture source and eligible channel: form views, completed subscriptions, delivered welcome and recovery messages, unique Shopify orders, discounts, refunds and opt-outs. Judge a higher signup rate alongside subsequent purchases and incentive expense. Do not credit the same order once to email and again to SMS in a combined business total. Keep a returned or partially refunded order in the net-sales reconciliation rather than counting its original value indefinitely.</p>
<p>Request a current proposal for the same eligible list, countries, email sends, SMS volume and support work. Include the cost of retaining a separate email provider if applicable. Pick Omnisend when a shared workflow fits your operators and passes the channel-skip checks; evaluate Attentive when its capture and channel arrangement fits your acquisition plan and the handoffs can be verified. Neither platform earns a numerical rating or guaranteed uplift from this comparison.</p>

<p>Primary documentation checked November 25, 2026. Exercises above are proposed checks, not observed vendor results.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
